  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nLow pressure will move southeast across IA today then across\nsouth/central IL tonight and across the Ohio River valley\nSaturday as it slows. Showers and at least a chance for \nthunderstorms will spread across the area this afternoon. As the\nlow approaches later today, the southern cwa may remain in the \nwarm sector allowing for a better chance of thunderstorms, a few\nof which may be strong to locally severe. Though it appears the\nbest chance of any severe thunderstorms will remain southwest \nof the local area.\n\nPeriods of rain will continue tonight into Saturday morning  \nalong with a continued chance for thunderstorms, which current \ntiming trends would suggest the best chance in the evening into \nthe early overnight of Saturday morning. The rain during this \ntime period will likely be moderate to heavy at times with \nprecipitable water values in the 2 inch range. With the system \nslowing as it moves southeast, at least scattered showers are \nexpected to continue Saturday and these may continue into \nSaturday night, especially across northwest IN. Models differ \nquite a bit for storm total rainfall but amounts in the 1 to 3 \ninch range look reasonable, highest amounts across the northern \ncwa. There may be some locally higher amounts as well.\n\nA prolonged period of northeast winds is expected, gusting to\n30-35 mph across southern Lake Michigan. This will allow for\nhigh waves and dangerous swimming conditions at Lake Michigan\nbeaches late tonight through early Monday morning and plan to\nissue a beach hazard statement with this morning's forecast.\n\nHigh pressure will bring dry weather back to the area early next\nweek with precip chances possibly returning late next week. cms",
